course: dictionary
lesson: 2891
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The sentence around the word tells you which meaning to use. «Your dose depends on your age and weight.» Here a dose is the amount of medicine you take at one time.
explain: «A pill box with the days of the week helps someone avoid a double dose.» A double dose means taking two amounts by mistake, and the pill box clue tells you this is about medicine. Ask yourself: what would fit here? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Dose can also mean an amount of something, like «a dose of sunshine». Here no medicine is involved, because the words of and sunshine show it is an amount. As a verb, to dose someone means to give them medicine, so the word is a doing word in that sentence.
fact: A dose is the amount of medicine you take at one time.
fact: A dose of something is an amount of it, like «a dose of sunshine».
fact: To dose someone means to give them medicine.
fact: The right dose is written on the packet, and an adult should give you your medicine.
fact: Taking more than the dose can be dangerous, even with common medicines.
exercise: «Your dose depends on your age and weight.» What is a dose in this sentence? | A walk around the park | A type of pill box | A kind of sunshine | *The amount of medicine you take at one time
exercise: Mum says, «Have a dose of sunshine on the beach today.» What does dose mean here? | A tablet you swallow | A sore throat | A bucket and spade | *An amount of sunshine
exercise: What does it mean to dose someone? | To wash their hair | To sleep next to them | *To give them medicine | To tell them a joke
test: «A pill box helps someone avoid a double dose.» What is a double dose? | A dose of sunshine and fresh air | A dose that is too small to work | A medicine that tastes nice | *Two amounts of medicine taken by mistake
test: «Children's medicines have special doses.» Which meaning of dose fits best here? | A type of pill that glows in the dark | A tiring walk to school | A sunny day at the seaside | *The amount of medicine given at one time
exam: Mia reads «Take a dose of sunshine every morning» and says dose must always mean medicine. Who is right? | She is right, because sunshine is a kind of medicine | She is wrong, because dose means to wash | She is right, dose always means medicine | *She is wrong, because here a dose is an amount of sunshine
